    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm mostly worried about the scalability if the collection grows. if we end up with a lot of uncatalogued or misidentified items in the interim, which system is better at handling that kind of disorder without breaking the whole hierarchy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with growing disorder, the inherent flexibility of the system is key, and this is where the structural differences become important. LCC tends to handle a sprawl of uncatalogued or misidentified items with more grace than DDC, especially if you are mixing in archival descriptions. DDC's strict numerical hierarchy can feel rigid when you have a sudden influx of items that don't neatly fit into an existing number range.\n\nLCC, being more character-based and allowing for extensive use of local or subsidiary classification notes, gives you more room to \"tuck\" an unknown item in temporarily without disrupting the established order of the known works. You can assign a provisional LCC number that signals \"needs review\" while keeping it logically near related materials, which is much easier to manage than forcing a completely anomalous item into a specific DDC slot.\n\nIn short, LCC offers a more porous structure for managing the chaos of a growing, messy collection, which is often the reality when dealing with historical finds. It allows you to quarantine the unknown items within the system more smoothly.",
      "source": "target",
