      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. you mentioned \"quarantining\" the unknown items. can you expand on that? i'm trying to visualize how a librarian would actually manage a bunch of items that are completely out of place or just seem to \"clutter\" the archive until they can be properly processed.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Quarantining\" in this context means creating a designated, temporary holding pattern within your cataloging metadata or physical shelving structure for items whose proper classification is currently unknown or too complex to assign immediately. You don't want a single, bizarrely placed manuscript to throw off the entire logical flow of your collection, which is what happens when you force a misfit into a rigid system like DDC.\n\nWith LCC, you can often use specific shelving ranges or metadata tags—like a designated \"Pending Review\" or \"Unclassified\" area within the collection record—to group these outliers. This keeps them physically or digitally separate from the well-cataloged treasures, preventing them from confusing future researchers. It's a controlled holding cell, not a permanent exile.\n\nThis practice is vital because it acknowledges the reality of discovery; you're not just shelving books, you're managing a slow, ongoing archaeological dig. You'll have a backlog, and the system needs to allow that backlog to exist without causing systemic collapse.",
